## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

Your code uses the **timeout enforcement** pattern — `Thread.Abort()` to kill work that exceeds a deadline. In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006). The fix is cooperative cancellation with `CancellationTokenSource`.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        return ExecuteWithTimeout(
            token => work(),  // wrap legacy non-cancellable work
            timeout);
    }

    // Prefer this overload — work can cooperate with cancellation
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            // Unwrap single-exception aggregates for cleaner stack traces
            throw ex.InnerExceptions[0];
        }
    }
}
```

### What Changed and Why

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(...)` | Task uses the thread pool; avoids manual thread management |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` | `task.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` | CTS handles the timeout; no manual join needed |
| `thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource(timeout)` | Cooperative cancellation — the **only** option in modern .NET |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | OCE is the cancellation exception in modern .NET |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | *(removed)* | No equivalent needed; cancellation is cooperative |

### Key Pitfalls

1. **Cancellation is NOT immediate.** Unlike `Thread.Abort`, the token doesn't inject an exception. The `Func<T>` overload (no token) will run to completion even after timeout — you just stop *waiting*. Always prefer the `Func<CancellationToken, T>` overload.

2. **The work must cooperate.** Insert checkpoints:
   ```csharp
   executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
   {
       foreach (var item in items)
       {
           token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
           Process(item);
       }
       return result;
   }, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
   ```

3. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`.** It allocates an internal timer — leaking it leaks the timer. Always `using`.

4. **Pass the token through the full call chain.** If your work calls `HttpClient`, `Stream.ReadAsync`, `DbCommand.ExecuteReaderAsync`, etc., pass the token to those calls too — they natively support cancellation.

5. **Uncooperative code (no token support).** If you truly cannot modify the work (e.g., third-party library), the task will keep running after timeout. The old `Thread.Abort` behavior — forcibly killing work — has **no equivalent** in modern .NET. For truly uncooperative code, move it to a child process and use `Process.Kill()`.
